2 CONT Database online.  Record for Balthaser Umbenhauer 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1737, Stephanus Umbenhauer immigrated from Bern, Switzerland, and purchased 220 acres (0.89 km2) from Thomas Penn. In 1819, Stephanus' grandson, Johann Thomas Umbenhauer, set aside 46 acres (190,000 m2) to be divided into 62 lo
2 CONC ts. On 24 August 1819, Peter Bennethum bought the first six lots. In January 1820, the town was named Bernville after Stephanus' birthplace.[7] The first house was built by Philip Filbert in 1820.[8] In 1828, the Union Canal was c
2 CONC ompleted, connecting the Susquehanna and Schuylkill rivers. Union Canal guard lock #36 and Lock #36 with a lift of 5.0 feet (1.5 m) is located at the southwest corner of Bernville, near where Northkill Creek empties into Tulpehock
2 CONC en Creek. An historic marker on Route 183 states: "The canal, at this point, followed the north bank of the Tulpehocken. A dam, two locks, and a mule bridge made it possible for boats to cross Northkill Creek. Nearby were grist mi
2 CONC lls, a lime kiln, and a tannery."[9] Due to the proximity of the Bernville Locks and the town's location on the canal, Bernville was a primary and prospering canal port.[10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851, the village of Bernville, discontented with Penn Township's road maintenance and community services, incorporated as a borough.[10] The borough included the original 62 lots, most of which measured 60 by 260 feet (18 by 7
2 CONC 9 m). In 1884, the Union Canal closed.[2] With no canal or railroad access, Bernville withered. In 1950, Bernville Borough annexed property from Penn Township at both ends of Main Street.
