1 TITL Dr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dr. Adam Simon Kuhn, who was a lawyer and a physician as well as an active layman during the formative years of Trinity Lutheran Church congregation, was buried in the front yard of the sanctuary. Dr. Kuhn emigrate
2 CONC d from Germany to Philadelphia in 1733. He subsequently moved to Lancaster and became a magistrate and an elder at Trinity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Kuhn was a member of the Union Fire Company No. 1, founded in 1742, and the oldest volunteer fire company in the United States. Included in the list of volunteers was James Buchanan, later President of the U.S., Edward Shippen
2 CONC , William Atlee, Gunsmith-Engineer William Henry, Innkeeper Mathias Slough, George Ross, a signer of the Declaration of Independence, Robert Fulton, Supreme Court Justice Jasper Yeated, Christopher Hager,  Amos, Jasper, Samuel and
2 CONC  Henry Slaymaker, Attorney-General Thomas E. Franklin, State Treasurer Henry Magraw and other distinguished men. Today the oldest fire company in America survives as a civic-historical association.     
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1744, John Musser and his wife sold to Dr. Adam Simon Kuhn 15 acres of land that became known as Adamstown, derived from “Adam’s Town”. That land was bound by what is now known as Church Street, John Street, Locust Street, and 
2 CONC Rockland Street. Following that acquisition, Kuhn laid out two main streets and several alleys. Next he divided the land into 48 building lots, most of them 64 feet wide and 255 feet deep. He disposed of the lots by conducting lot
2 CONC teries, with the holder of the winning ticket receiving a “free lot”. Five years later he sold to James Hamilton the rights to collect ground rents on his lots. Taking the example of Dr. Kuhn, John Musser laid out an area known as
2 CONC  Mussertown, and Samuel Bethel laid out Betheltown. Eventually these towns were brought into the borough, but the strange pattern of street grids continues to infuriate motorists as well as honor the rugged individualism of Lancas
2 CONC ter’s founders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1763 Dr. Kuhn built a large house on East King Street, adjacent to his Adamstown development. In 1788 it was sold and became the Indian Queen Tavern. It existed as a hotel until 1883 when it was razed to make way for the Easter
2 CONC n Market House, which is still standing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Adam Simon Kuhn and his wife produced three sons, Adam, John and Frederick. All became physicians. John was a surgeon in the Revolutionary army. Especially well recognized is Adam, who was appointed Professor of Materia Medica
2 CONC  and Botany at the College of Philadelphia, being the third member of Penn’s first medical facility. His father died in 1780, and his white grave stone is the center one of three located on the church’s west exterior wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Material collected from “The Heritage of Lancaster”, University of Pennsylvania Archives, lancasterhistory.org, ushistory.org, and innercitygroupworks.org
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT “A Biographical History of Lancaster Co. Being a History of Early Settlers and Eminent Men of the County” by Alex. Harris, 1827 Lancaster, Pa.: Elias Barr & Co., 1872
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 355. DR. ADAM SIMON KUHN, son of JOHN CHRISTOPHER KUHN, was a native of a small town of Swabia, near Heilbron, on the Neckar.  He came with his father to Philadelphia in 1733.  He was a magistrate of the borough of Lancaster f
2 CONC or many, and an elder of Trinity Lutheran Church.(pg. 356)  There is a tablet in the Lutheran Church, perpetuating his memory.  DR. JOHN KUHN, DR. FREDERICK KUHN, and DR. ADAM KUHN were sons of the above.  DR. JOHN KUHN graduated 
2 CONC at the Academy of Pa., and afterwards at the University of Edinburgh.  He was a surgeon in the Revolutionary Army.  He first located at Reading, and married a MISS JONES of that place, and afterwards returned to Lancaster, where h
2 CONC e practiced till his death.  He was one of the leading physicians of Lancaster.  DR. FREDERICK KUHN was also one of the leading physicians of Lancaster.   He served for some time as associate judge of the courts.  He died April 1,
2 CONC  1816, in the 68th year of his age.  DR. ADAM KUHN was professor of botany and materia medica in the University of Pa.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/189435415/adam-simon-kuhn Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 19 2019, 5:12:08 UTC''
