1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Adam Kuhn''' (28 November 1741 – 5 July 1817) was an American physician and naturalist, and one of the earliest professors of medicine in a North American university.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuhn was born in Germantown, Province of Pennsylvania, son of German immigrant parents. He studied medicine under his father, Dr. Adam Simon Kuhn.[1] Then he went to Sweden and studied medicine and natural history 1761–1764 at Upp
2 CONC sala University, where he was probably the only American student of Carl Linnaeus. He continued his studies at the University of Edinburgh, where he graduated as M.D. in June 1767, and published his thesis, De Lavatione frigida.[1
2 CONC ] Returning to America, he practiced as a physician in Philadelphia and was 1768–1789 professor of Materia medica and 1789–1797 of the Theory and Practice of Medicine at the Medical School of the College of Philadelphia (later the
2 CONC  University of Pennsylvania), founded in 1765 as the first faculty of Medicine in the thirteen colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuhn was a physician of the Pennsylvania Hospital from May 1775, until January 1798. He was one of the founders of the College of Physicians of Philadelphia in 1787, and was its president from July 1808 until his death.[1] His stu
2 CONC dents included Valentine Seaman, who mapped yellow fever mortality patterns in New York and introduced the smallpox vaccine to the United States in 1799.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Rush wrote in his autobiography that Kuhn, after the death of Dr John Jones in June 1791, was considered the leading physician in Philadelphia and the one favored by "the principal officers of the general government". He 
2 CONC treated "Washington Custes" (George Washington Custis, the son of Washington's stepson, see Martha Washington) and functioned as the family physician of George Washington. There is, however, no evidence that he actually treated th
2 CONC e President.[3] [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_Kuhn Wikipedia] 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Adam Kuhn was born on November 17, 1741 (Old Style) in Germantown, Philadelphia. His grandfather, John Christopher Kuhn, and his father, Adam Simon Kuhn, were both natives of Farfeld, a small town near Heilbronn, Germany. They imm
2 CONC igrated to Philadelphia in 1733. Kuhn’s father, an educated man, was a successful and skillful medical practitioner who subsequently removed to Lancaster to become a magistrate and an elder of the Lutheran Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuhn’s early studies of medicine were with his father. In 1761 he went to Norway and Sweden to study botany under Linnaeus and other professors of the University of Upsala. He was there until 1764. After residing in London for one
2 CONC  year, Kuhn went to Edinburgh, where he received his degree of Doctor of Medicine on June 12, 1767.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1768, after visiting France, Holland, and Germany, Kuhn returned to America, where he was immediately appointed professor of materia medica and botany in the Medical Department of the College of Philadelphia, becoming t
2 CONC he third member of Penn’s first medical faculty. He commenced his first course of lectures in May of 1768, three months after his arrival from Europe. Kuhn continued in this position until 1789, when he was made professor of the t
2 CONC heory and practice of medicine. After the 1791 union of the College of Philadelphia and the University of the State of Pennsylvania as the University of Pennsylvania, he continued in this same chair until his resignation in 1797. 
2 CONC In 1806, he was elected a trustee, but declined to serve.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January of 1774, Kuhn was one of the physicians of the Society for Inoculating the Poor. He was one of the founders of the College of Physicians, the first of its kind in America. He served as its President in 1808. He was also
2 CONC  a member of the American Philosophical Society, and an honorary member of the Massachusetts Medical Society. Kuhn was twice elected to the medical staff of the Pennsylvania Hospital, serving from 1774 to 1781 and from 1782 to 179
2 CONC 8, when he resigned. He was also a well-known botanist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuhn had a profound talent for observation. His peers described him as studious, abstemious in his diet, and neat in his person. He married Elizabeth, daughter of Isaac Hartman, on the Island of St. Croix, West Indies, in May of 1
2 CONC 780, and had two sons. Kuhn died in Philadelphia on July 5, 1817, at the age of seventy-six. [https://archives.upenn.edu/exhibits/penn-people/biography/adam-kuhn Archive/UofPenn] 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-1/myheritage-family-trees?itemId=133414441-1-212&action=showRecord&indId=externalindividual-78a75925d340db192c775e461ae483ba&mrid=341260f6bb06f64b92240b52f38480d7 MyHer
2 CONC itage Family Trees] via son [http://www.geni.com/profile-34628002436 Hartman Kuhn] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 9 2015, 18:04:26 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT son of Adam Simon Kuhn & Anna Maria Schrack
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/80508973/adam-kuhn Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 19 2019, 5:12:08 UTC'' 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Kuhn (November 17, 1741–July 5, 1817) was born in Germantown. He studied medicine with his father, Adam Simon Kuhn, a skilled medical practitioner and elder of the Lutheran Church. At the age of 20, he studied Botany under Ca
2 CONC rolus Linnaeus and other professors of the University of Upsala. He received his degree of Doctor of Medicine in Edinburgh on June 12, 1767.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brothers were Dr. John Kuhn (a surgeon in the Revolutionary army) and Dr. Frederick Kuhn.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1768, he returned to America, and was appointed Professor of Materia Medica and Botany in the College of Philadelphia, being the third member of Penn's first medical faculty. In 1789, he was made Professor of the Theory
2 CONC  and Practice of Medicine (in 1792 the College was reconstituted as the University of Pennsylvania). Among his students was Philip Syng Physick.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January of 1774, Kuhn was one of the physicians of the Society for Inoculating the Poor. He was a founder and President (succeeding William Shippen) of the College of Physicians, the first of its kind in America. He was also a 
2 CONC member of the American Philosophical Society. Kuhn was twice elected to the Medical Staff of the Pennsylvania Hospital. [http://www.ushistory.org/germantown/people/kuhn.htm USHistory.org]
