2 CONT  In addition, there were some descendants of Johann Michael Propst, the immigrant, who settled in North Carolina.  Dr. Heinrich Propst, MD (not Dr. Henry Propst, DVM), born abt 1750-1755, married Catherine Abendschon in Anson Coun
2 CONC ty, and lived later in Cabarrus County.  His early life has been somewhat of a mystery, according to a Cabarrus County history.  Was he a native of Bad Gandershiem, Germany, or of Pennsylvania, or of Lincoln County, NC?  Other rec
2 CONC ords show him as a son of Johann Michael Propst, Jr.; however, the most credible records do not show him there. Walter Eye, in his pedigree register of the Propst family, does not show him anywhere!
2 CONT
2 CONT  GENDEX states that he was born in 1740, in Schwaigern, Wuerttemberg, Germany, but that may have been the other Dr. Henry Propst, DVM.
2 CONT
2 CONT  At the height of the smallpox epidemic in Cabarrus County, NC, in the mid-1780s, one Heinrich Propst, physician, is recorded as having treated patients, per  Rouse's Cabarrus County history.  He practiced medicine in the German c
2 CONC ommunity between Coldwater and Buffalo Creeks, but was he really a physician?.  A history of Mecklenberg County, NC, reflects this:
2 CONT
2 CONT   "Epidemic of smallpox in 1780 brought by British and American armies. Catherine Blackwelder, of Cabarrus, acted as a nurse and no doubt saved many lives by her care and self-sacrifice. Some of those who were the recipients of he
2 CONC r attentions paid her, but the money was almost worthless continental currency all the time, so that she never received any adequate compensation for her heroic efforts to save the lives of her friends and neighbors. Henry Propst,
2 CONC  of Cabarrus, in 1780, rendered an account against one of neighbors for 'four fisicks and rideing.' He was not a physician, but no doubt had some knowledge of medicine."
2 CONT
2 CONT  Was he a doctor to the Hessian soldiers who took leave of the British troops at Camden, SC, and settled in Cabarrus County?  He was fluent in German, and a successful physician or pseudo-physician.  Since he was actively practici
2 CONC ng medicine in 1780, he must have been at least 25 years old at that time, so was born sometime before about 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In the spring of 1808, Dr. Propst was called to the home of a sick friend.  The patient was given medical assistance, and then Dr. Propst began the long trip back home by horseback.  Not too far from his home, a violent electrica
2 CONC l and rain storm forced the rider under the branches of a giant oak tree.  A bolt of lightning struck the tree and instantly killed him.
2 CONT
