2 CONT In addition, there were some descendants of Johann Michael Propst, the
2 CONT immigrant, who settled in North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Heinrich Propst, MD (not Dr. Henry Propst, DVM), born abt 1750-1755, married Catherine Abendschon in Anson County, and lived later in Cabarrus County.
2 CONT
2 CONT His early life has been somewhat of a mystery, according to Cabarrus County history. Was he a native of Bad Gandershiem, Germany, or of Pennsylvania, or of Lincoln County, NC?
2 CONT
2 CONT Other records show him as a son of Johann Michael Propst, Jr.; however, the most credible records do not show him there. Walter Eye, in his pedigree register of the Propst family, does not show him anywhere!
2 CONT
2 CONT GENDEX states that he was born in 1740, in Schwaigern, Wuerttemberg,
2 CONT Germany, but that may have been the other Dr. Henry Propst, DVM.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the height of the smallpox epidemic in Cabarrus County, NC, in the
2 CONT mid-1780s, one Heinrich Propst, physician, is recorded as having treated patients, per Rouse's Cabarrus County history. He practiced medicine in the German community between Coldwater and Buffalo Creeks, but was he really a physic
2 CONC ian?. A history of Mecklenberg County, NC, reflects this:
2 CONT "Epidemic of smallpox in 1780 brought by British and American armies.
2 CONT Catherine Blackwelder, of Cabarrus, acted as a nurse and no doubt saved many lives by her care and self-sacrifice. Some of those who were the recipients of her attentions paid her, but the money was almost worthless continental cu
2 CONC rrency all the time so that she never received any adequate compensation for her heroic efforts to save the lives of her friends and neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Propst, of Cabarrus, in 1780, rendered an account against one of his neighbors for 'four fisicks and rideing.' He was not a physician, but no doubt had some knowledge of medicine."
2 CONT
2 CONT Was he a doctor to the Hessian soldiers who took leave of the British
2 CONT troops at Camden, SC, and settled in Cabarrus County?
2 CONT
2 CONT He was fluent in German, and a successful physician or pseudo-physician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since he was actively practicing medicine in 1780, he must have been at least 25 years old at that time, so was born sometime before about 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1808, Dr. Propst was called to the home of a sick
2 CONT friend. The patient was given medical assistance, and then Dr. Propst began the long trip back home by horseback. Not too far from his home, a violent electrical and rain storm forced the rider under the branches of a giant oak tr
2 CONC ee. A bolt of lightning struck the tree and instantly killed him.
2 CONT
