2 CONC t overland to CA where he engaged in mining for gold. He returned to IA in 1853 and lived there until 1860; when, finding neighbors too many and near to be endured, he started for the wilds of OR. He settled finally in Jackson Co.
2 CONC , OR about 1862. John was in Oregon, with his cattle and savage bear dogs, his hair long and white; a patriarch as rough and rugged and intractable, and honest and sincere, as the mountains which surrounded him. [History of Buchan
2 CONC an County, Iowa, pp. 48, 202.] John was a Judge of Election in Township No. 1, Buchanan Co., IA in 1848. At first the townships were known simply by number. [History of Buchanan County, Iowa, p. 62.]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was in the 1870 & 1880 Census for Jackson Co., OR.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Putnam Co., Indiana (Lewis Pub. Co., 1887), p. 221. History of Buchanan Co., Iowa (Williams Bros., 1881), pp. 42, 45, 48, 52, 62, 282. Census Records: 1830 Putnam Co,m IN, p. 217; 1840 Putnam Co., IN, p. 843; 1850 Bucha
2 CONC nan Co., IA, p. 157.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was listed as age 48 in the 1850 Census of Buchanan Co., IA; occupation, farmer; birthplace, VA; Margaret was also listed as 48; birthplace, VA. The following children were listed: Mary Obenchain, age 24; Barkley Obenchain, a
2 CONC ge 23, farmer; Davis Obenchain, age 20, labourer; James Obenchain, age 18, labourer; George W. Obenchain, age 16, labourer; John Obenchain, age 8; & Madison Obenchain, age 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT   Julia Ann Obenchain Gritton (1824 - 1884)*
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Obenchain: A Family, A Mountain, A Road 
2 CONT Their name christens a mountain and a road northeast of Medford. They planted Southern Oregon roots in a rugged area of deep springs, jagged rocks, oak, pine, and well-timbered land with good soil.  Their name is Obenchain.
2 CONT Family roots go back to Botecourt County, Virginia, where John W. and Margaret Obenchain, descendants of German immigrants, became parents of their first son, Bartlett, on September 22, 1827. Two years later, in 1829, the family m
2 CONC oved west, first to Indiana, then to Illinois in 1842, and finally to Iowa in 1844. In 1861, the Obenchain family, which now included four more sons - John Allen, Madison, James, and George headed for Oregon.
2 CONT The Obenchains' Oregon homestead was situated on what was then a narrow dirt wagon trail just over the mountain's summit, halfWay between Eagle Point and the later community of Butte Falls. With the construction in 1863 of the mil
2 CONC itary road between Fort Klamath and Jacksonville along portions of the same route, the enterprising couple made their homestead a stage stop and way station. They also had five more children.
2 CONT Bartlett Obenchain had married Nancy Morse in Iowa in 1850; she was a cousin of telegraph inventor Samuel F.B. Morse. They migrated west with the elder Obenchains, the younger couple settling and farming on more than 100 acres a m
2 CONC ile west of Central Point.
2 CONT Eventually, the family fanned out to many parts of Southern Oregon, continuing its tradition of farming, hard work, and community involvement.
2 CONT Bartlett's brother, John Allen, became postmaster when the Big Butte Post
2 CONT Office opened at the way station on October 6, 1888. It was a post he held
2 CONT until March 31, 1908. John Allen, father of five daughters, died on June 16, 1913, in Butte Falls.
2 CONT His great-great-granddaughter, Katherine Gott Wilkinson of Portland, is documenting the Obenchain family genealogy. Lucinda, her great-grandmother and John Allen's daughter, married William Henry Chambers, the grandson of Aaron Ch
2 CONC ambers, who built the Chambers House in Jacksonville. According to Wilkinson, another Obenchain relative, Maldoren Obenchain, moved to Klamath Falls in the late nineteenth century and became sheriff there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Jacksonville Cemetery  Jacksonville Jackson County Oregon, USA
