2 CONT Michael Zirkle gave Revolutionary War service in Dunmore militia in 1776 Listed are: Andrew Cirkle, Michael Cirkle, Peter Cirkle (Revolutionary War Records, Volume 1, Virginia by Gaius Marcus Brumbaugh, Washington, D.C. 1936, page
2 CONC  607, "A List of the Men's Names in Dunmore County Militia Under the Command of Capt. Jacob Holman." Second reference: "Dunmore County, Papers 1775-1776 (21127), 65-2550, Virginia State Library) During the Revolutionary War "Durin
2 CONC g the Revolutionary War "Michael joined the Dunmore County Militia under the command of Captain Jacob Holeman along with his brothers Andrew and Peter. Later this militia was called into service and became part of the 8th Virginia
2 CONC  Regiment of Foot of the Continental Line under the leadership of Colonel(later Brigadier General) John Peter Gabriel Muhlenberg. The 8th Virginia took part in battles in South Carolina, most notably the Battle of Charleston and o
2 CONC f Fort Moultrie in which a British Naval force of 10 ships was soundly defeated.  The weather and conditions in the south were not kind to the regiment and fully half of them became quite ill with fever and malaria. A great deal o
2 CONC f them were transported back home to Woodstock to be cared for by the local people. The rest of the regiment marched north a short while later to join General Washington's Northern Army and fought at Brandywine, White Marsh and Ge
2 CONC rmantown. They later went into winter camp at Valley Forge.   The weather and conditions in the south were not kind to the regiment and fully half of them became quite ill with fever and malaria. A great deal of them were transpor
2 CONC ted back home to Woodstock to be cared for by the local people. The rest of the regiment marched north a short while later to join General Washington's Northern Army and fought at Brandywine, White Marsh and Germantown. They later
2 CONC  went into winter camp at Valley Forge. 
2 CONT
