1 TITL Brig. Gen.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel Ovenshine was born on April 2, 1843 in Philadelphia. He was studying to become a lawyer when he was interrupted by the outbreak of the Civil War. On September 25, 1861 he was appointed first lieutenant in th
2 CONC e 5th U.S. Infantry Regiment. He served in Kansas and New Mexico and ended the war as a captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine stayed with the 5th U.S. Infantry during the Indian Wars. His unit formed the vanguard of Alfred H. Terry's column during the aftermath of the Battle of Little Bighorn and served in Nelson A. Miles' force at the Battle o
2 CONC f Bearpaw Mountain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was promoted to major in the 23rd United States Infantry on July 10, 1885 and commanded the post of Fort Davis in 1890. He was promoted to lieutenant colonel of the 15th United States Infantry Regiment on January 31, 189
2 CONC 1 and colonel of the 23rd United States Infantry Regiment on April 26, 1895.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Ovenshine and the 23rd U.S. Infantry were sent to the Philippines during the Spanish-American War as part of Wesley Merritt's Eighth Army Corps. The fighting against the Spanish had already subsided by the time Ovenshine a
2 CONC rrived, but hostilities between the U.S. Army and Filipinos were then growing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was promoted to brigadier general of volunteers by the end of September 1898 and took command of the 2nd Brigade in Arthur MacArthur's 1st Division of Eighth Corps. Ovenshine led his brigade during the battle of Manila i
2 CONC n 1899 and during the battle of Zapote Bridge. He received a Silver Star for his actions in these two battles.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 18, 1899 he was promoted to brigadier general in the U.S. Army and two days later retired.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Source: Wikipedia as of 7 August 2015 edit)* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/155633419/samuel-ovenshine Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 15 2019, 3:45:20 UT
2 CONC C''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Arlington website:
2 CONT Samuel Ovenshine was born in Philadelphia on April 2, 1843. His father died when he was 16, and when the Civil War broke out 18-year-old Ovenshine was studying law in Baltimore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following President Abraham Lincoln's call for 75,000 volunteers to quell the rebellion against the government, Ovenshine was appointed from Maryland, Second Lieutenant, 5th United States Infantry on August 5, 1861. His entire ser
2 CONC vice in the Civil War was with that regiment, first in Kansas and later New Mexico. The regiment prevented those regions from joining the Confederacy and provided protection against Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was promoted to First Lieutenant on September 25, 1861; Captain on March 30, 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine participated in many of the important Indian campaigns of the 19th Century. They included the Black Kettle Campaign of 1868-69, under the command of Major General Philip H. Sheridan in what is now Oklahoma; and The Red R
2 CONC iver War in 1874 against the Northern Cheyennes and Sioux in Montana.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of the 5th Infantry, Ovenshine was with the first division of reinforcements to arrive in Montana after the foibles of General George A. Custer at the Battle of Little Big Horn, 1876.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was promoted to Major, 23rd United States Infantry on July 10, 1885; Lieutenant Colonel, 15th United States Infantry on January 31, 1891; and Colonel, 23rd United States, April 26, 1895.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was twice awarded the Silver Star for gallantry in action during the Philippine Insurrection, following the capture of Manila on February 5, 1899 and the Battle of Zapote River on June 13, 1899.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Zapote River, Ovenshine commanded one of two provisional brigades that fought one of the largest and fiercest battles of the war. The fighting began when 1,000 Filipinos ambushed the brigades seven miles south of Manila. The Am
2 CONC erican troops had to break through the enemy flank to gain control of the river.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his official report, Brigadier General Arthur MacArthur (father of Douglas MacArthur) stated: "The invincible composure of Colonel Ovenshine, during an exposure in dangerous space for more than an hour, was conspicuous and very
2 CONC  inspiring to the troops; and the efficient manner in which he took advantage of opportunities as they arose during the varying aspects of the fight was of great practical value in determining the result."
2 CONT
2 CONT Major General Henry W. Lawton reported: "(Colonel) Ovenshine, riding back and forth not over 150 yards from the enemy's entrenchments, directing his command, was certainly an inspiring example to his men."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovenshine was promoted to Brigadier General on October 18, 1899. He retired on October 20, 1899. Aside from the Silver Star, he was awarded the following service medals: Civil War, Indian Wars, War with Spain, Philippine Insurrect
2 CONC ion and Philippine Congressional Medal.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Ovenshine and his wife, Sallie, who had seven children, made their home in Washington D.C.  He died there on July 5, 1932. He was buried with full military honors in Section 1 of Arlington National Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following family members are also buried in this gravesite:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sallie Yeatman Ovenshine. Wife of Samuel Ovenshine. 1848-1918.
2 CONT
2 CONT Englebert Glover Ovenshine, Colonel, United States Army. Son of Samuel
2 CONT Ovenshine. September 4, 1871-January 6, 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethel Keyes Ovenshine, wife of Englebert Glover Ovenshine. December 3,
2 CONT 1884-August 13, 1986.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sallie Yeatman Ovenshine. Daughter of Samuel Ovenshine. 1885-1960.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter, Harriet Ovenshine Weeks, was married to an Army officer and is buried in Section 1 with her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another daughter, Mary Aston Ovenshine Norvell, wife of Colonel Guy Stevens Norvell, United States Army, is also buried in Arlington with her husband. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Ovenshine
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/KCCV-DQ7
2 CONT
