2 CONT *  5.  (3). Sarah                                   d.       1818         m. Henry Hawes
2 CONT
2 CONT ——
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah is the daughter of Roger and Hannah (Smith) Dyer. Sarah was the wife of Maj. Robert Davis. They had at least one son, John Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah was captured by Indians at Fort Seybert.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From "A History of Pendleton County, West Virginia", by Oren Frederic Morton:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Dyer is said to have been instrumental in effecting the recovery of his sister Sarah Hawes, whose captivity lasted three and a half years. She thought better of the Indians than of the French who sometimes visited the villag
2 CONC e. There was usually an abundance to eat, but in time of scarcity colt steak was prominent on the Indian bill of fare, and to this she demurred. But Killbuck asked her why she should have prejudice against an animal that eats only
2 CONC  clean food, when all palefaces were fond of eating the flesh of the hog, an animal that searches in all manner of filth for something to eat. Her captivity worked some change in her appearance and manner, and when she returned he
2 CONC r little daughter was not for a while willing to own her, but at length accepted the fact of identity. Her husband died either before her return or shortly thereafter, and then she married Robert Davis
2 CONT
