2 CONT *doesn't refer to her directly by name but rather by inference to his two sons and two daughters - note that her sister Catern and brothers Jacob and Frederick are mentioned by name. Johanna was provided for under the will to the 
2 CONC extent of her one-fourth particapation in the remainder of the will after specific bequests.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a possibility that at the time Nicholas was preparing his will in 1769 there was uncertainity of the whereabouts of his daughter Maria Johanna. The following is taken from the FORT SEYBERT MASSACRE, Ft Seybert by Mary L K
2 CONC eister Talbot, pg 52 and quoted in part as follows; "after a journey of nine days the band reached Indian village in the Ohio near Chillicothe. The captives whose names are known were Nicholas Seybert, James Dyer, Mrs Hawes, Miss 
2 CONC Peterson and a Miss Hevener was a refrence to a sister Maria Caterina or more likely Maria Johanna Hevener. Pam's opinion - 1 - Nicholas' will confirms he had 2 sons and 2 daughters. 2 - Nicholas did include Maria Johanna in his w
2 CONC ill of 1769 - she was alive and well. 3 - Maria Johanna was married to George Coil, who was listed on Nicholas' estate settlement, named George Gile on the original document and as Geo. Coiles on the clerk's record. 4 - Nicholas c
2 CONC rossed out his daughter Hannah Gile in the original will and the clerk did not include this in the court transcription--because Hannah did not require any assets from her father as she was married. 5 - Maria Johanna Hevener Coil/G
2 CONC ile/Kile died in OH about 1837. 6 - No where in the Indian raid does it state - the captives died or the first name of Miss Hevener. It is a hard stretch to think "Miss Hevener" even refers to Hannah. It could have easily been her
2 CONC  sister Catern. The writers chose to assume it was Maria Johanna because they could not find her in the court records. The writers never looked at the real source, which is the original will of Nicholas Hevener.
2 CONT
2 CONT Executrix of husband's will in 1794
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1823, Hannah signed POA for William Sinnett (her son-in-law, Mary's husband) to go back to VA to settle herdeceased husband's estate. The record was filed in Pendleton Co, WV Deed Bk 9, page 119. The affidavit was signed in Fra
2 CONC nklin Co, OH and witnessed by William Duff, JOP fro Franklin Co, OH. William Sinnett and his wife Mary (d/o George Sr & Hannah) signed the POA and the signatures were witnessed by Arthur O'Harra and William Woods...State of OH, Co
2 CONC unty of Franklin.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Johannah (Mary Hannah) Coil formerly Hevener Born about 1752 in Tulpehocken, Lancaster County (now Berks County), Pennsylvania Colony Ancestors  Daughter of Nicholas William Hevener and Anna Elizabeth (Seybert) Hevener  Sist
2 CONC er of Jacob Havener, Frederick (Hefner) Hevener Sr., Baltasar Hevener and Margaret Elizabeth (Hevener) Rexrode
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of George Coil — married [date unknown] [location unknown]  Descendants  Mother of Catherine Coil, Georg Coil, Barbara Sarah (Coil) Fisher, Mary Hannah (Coil) Mullenax and Maria Elizabetha (Coil) Sinnett  Died before Feb 1837
2 CONC  in Chariton, Chariton County, Missouri, United States
2 CONT
