2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT From the Ritchie County Weekly Review in 1973 A Pioneer Gone Death of George Sinnett - On Wendesday evening, October 23, 1895, George Sinnett, a venerable and remarkable man died at his home at this place.  In his demise, Ritche C
2 CONC ounty loses a pioneer and a landmark.  Mr. Sinnett was born on the 25th day of march 1799 and consequently if he had lived till the next anniversary ofhis birth date, would have been 97 years old.  This appears to be pretty defini
2 CONC tely settled as to his age by his own statements while living, and the family records, although many think he is at least 103 years old.  His oldest daughter, Mrs. Rexroad, is still living at the age of 77, and she thinks he was 2
2 CONC 5 years old when he married her mother.  Patrick Sinnett, the father of the deceased, bought the farm now in the suburbs of Harrisville and moved on it in the year 1815.  There for 80 years the subject of this sketch lived, until 
2 CONC in the ripemess of extreme age, he died where for so long he had lived.   As nearly as can be ascertained he had been a member of the Baptist Church for 70 years.  He united with what is now the Harrisville church during the pasto
2 CONC rage of the Elder John Drake. who was pastor of the church in 1825.  The church then called Mab Zeal.  Mr. Sinnett was married twice.  By his first wife he had 6 children, four sons, two daughters; by his second wife he had 3 chil
2 CONC dren.  All of these are yet living but one. Mr. Sinnett's ailment might be said to merely old age.  Up to within the year or two back, he was quite hale and hearty, but lately broken down entirely.  For several weeks before his de
2 CONC ath, he gradually sank until he passed away entirely.  --Ritchie County Weekly Echo. (typed into computer - March 13, 1999 by LER)
