1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Military enlistment''': Milit-Beg - June 14 1861
2 CONT * '''Military discharge''': Milit-End - June 10 1865
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Verona farmer considered bravest Confederate...
2 CONT During the battle of Cedar Mountain near Culpeper, a small contingent of confederates from Virginia Company H, 10th Va. Infantry found themselves outnumbered by a much larger union force.
2 CONT In the Aug. 9, 1862 battle, a 22-year-old Verona farmer and crack marksman named Isaac "Ike" Keister took either a bullet or shrapnel to the head and went down.
2 CONT Horrified, his comrades tried to drag his lifeless body off the field, but Keister suddenly regained consciousness, angrily rose to his feet and with his face drenched in blood killed not only the Yankee who shot him, but also a U
2 CONC nion commander.
2 CONT Then, refusing all offers of assistance, Keister walked home to recover from his wound.
2 CONT A Pendleton County now West Virginia native but lifelong Verona resident, Ike Keister was considered by many to be the Confederate army's most audacious and daring soldier. On May 12 1864, after returning to his unit and fighting 
2 CONC with a bald spot on his partially-healed head, Keister's Lieutenant ordered all weapons dropped in surrender to a much larger Union battalion at Spotsylvania Courthouse.
2 CONT "Just at that moment I heard the report of a musket," former Lt. J.W. Mauck told the Rockingham Register newspaper in 1903, "and on looking around I saw it was Keister who had fired."
2 CONT The angry Mauck marched Keister to the rear and admonished him, "Ike, why did you kill that Yankee after my telling you to cease firing, throw down your arms and surrender?"
2 CONT Keister responded "I-gaddie, Lieutenant, I was not going to throw down my gun with a load in it."
2 CONT Keister and the rest of his fellow rebels were subsequently taken prisoner to Fort Delaware, on Pea Patch Island on the Delaware River, where they remained for 13 months. Mauck recalled that as an officer he had secured early rele
2 CONC ase from the prison but did not want to leave without informing his fellow soldiers still inside.
2 CONT Once out he somehow got a note to Keister, telling him to "play muskrat" and come out to see him.
2 CONT Under the cover of darkness, Keister belly-crawled an estimated 500 yards through a sewer pipe, spent the night in the woods talking to his Lieutenant, then slithered back into prison before daybreak. "Had he been detected by the 
2 CONC guards it would have been sure death," Mauck reported, adding that prison officers would have put him in "the black hole" or hanged him by his thumbs if caught.
2 CONT Keister continued his bold exploits even after his release from the Union prison.
2 CONT In another unnamed battle, he and a number of his confederates were again captured and he reportedly became so enraged over the "triumphant air" of his northern captors he was somehow able to fire his rifle. Although no one was hi
2 CONC t, the fire drew southern reinforcements and his unit was re-taken from the Yankees because of it.
2 CONT A fellow soldier named J. Minnich wrote in the Old Dominion Sun newspaper in 1903 that "Ike Keister was one of the very best soldiers in the ranks I ever saw." Another anonymous soldier declared in that paper that "Keister was one
2 CONC  of the kind made from the whole cloth, although he has a bare place in his scalp."
2 CONT In 1867, Keister married Mary Cline Byers her second marriage and resumed farming again on Middle River just north of Verona. There they raised four sons, two daughters and two step-daughters. Mary died September 29, 1909 at age 7
2 CONC 2.
2 CONT The Confederate army's allegedly boldest private died after a brief illness one day shy of his 76th birthday on Feb. 24, 1916.
2 CONT Despite being an almost lifetime member of Verona Evangelical United Brethren Church, Keister was buried in Augusta Stone Church's cemetery in Fort Defiance 
