1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Reminesescenses of a journey from 1835 to 1837, written out by an old man at the solicitation and
2 CONT for the pleasure of his grandchildren.  By Joseph L. Schooley
2 CONT
2 CONT (the first several pages cover his travels to Texas and his return to New York City).
2 CONT We landed on the 4th of March, 1837, the day Martin Van Buren was inaugerated President
2 CONT of the United States. I stopped for a short time in the city and started for my old home in Easton,
2 CONT Pa. where I remained until about the 18th of April when I left for Wisconsin by way of New York
2 CONT and Buffalo. On the first day of May we found the harbor closed with ice, and no boats running as
2 CONT yet. We took stage for Erie , Pa. and turned over twice. The last time I was marred and brusied
2 CONT badly. At Erie we took a boat for Detroit. I stopped at Toledo, a town just in its infancy with a few
2 CONT houses scattered here and there. When we got to Detroit we learned that Mackinaw straits were
2 CONT not open yet and no propsect of openning soon. A number of us men concluded not to wait in
2 CONT Detroit for the openning of the straits and hired a team to carry us and baggage to Chicago, a new
2 CONT town of only a few years growth. About six men started with the team over the worse road I ever
2 CONT travelled - chick up and down and in and out of holeswas continuous. Walking was better than
2 CONT riding which was done most of the way. When we got to Clinton sore and tired I abandoned that
2 CONT way of locomotion and stopped there until I heardof the openning of the straits when I returned to
2 CONT Detroit and took passage to Green Bay on the steamer Michigan. I think it was the second boat
2 CONT leaving that place for the upper lakes. And on the 12th day of June, 1837 we landed in Green Bay,
2 CONT a little over 18 months after leaving New York for Texas.
2 CONT Green Bay was then a small stragling town with Ft. Howard right across the river garrisoned
2 CONT by troops. One house below the slough opposite Judge Arndt's place occupied by the family of
2 CONT Daniel Hubbard. A few Canadians lived above the sloughs. Col. Ryan and Col. Johns occupied
2 CONT houses belonging to the Fort. So much for the city of Ft. Howard at that time. Navarino and Astor
2 CONT is the presetn Green Bay at tthat time and estended a little east of the old Whitney homestead on
2 CONT Main St. to Washington St. and south to Judge Low place. Same on Adams St. and a few
2 CONT scattering building in Navarion and Astor. Astor House and dock were building then. Judge Law's
2 CONT house was built since. The Methodist church stood where the French Catholic church is and C. C.
2 CONT P. Arndt's house on the corner adjoining the Astor Hall on the south side of the park. Sold
2 CONT afterwards to the Hollanders. The Presbyterians held their meetings in a small building on Walnut
2 CONT Street near Adams - preacher Steven Pact. The old Washington House kept by Uncle Tommy
2 CONT Green stood where the Beaumont house is. The Navarino Hotel kept by Parmely and Driggswhere
2 CONT Uncle Franks block is.
2 CONT When I landed in Green Bay I though I had got among a community of distingushed men.
2 CONT Judges and Colenel without number. By couresty nothing lessthan Colnel except in Ft. Howard
2 CONT where they had Captains, Lieutenants and privates who were very jealous of being addressed
2 CONT otherwise than by their titles.
2 CONT In 1839 I and many other of this place located claims on the east shore of Lake Winnebago
2 CONT - the Eden of North Wisconsin which was put in market the spring of 1840.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The History of Winnebago County & The Fox River Valley" by R. G. Thwaites which
2 CONT appeared in serial form in the Oshkosh Times in 1877
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph L. Schooley was the next addition to the infant settlement. Mr. Schooley was born in
2 CONT Easton, Pa., in 1813 and at the age of 15 years entered the office of The Easton Argus, where he
2 CONT learned the tyographic art. The Argus, which is still inexistance, was then conducted by Wygant &
2 CONT Ineas, and was a prominent democratic organ in those early days of American journalism. He was
2 CONT afterwards a typo on the Philadelphia Post, and other Pennsylvania papers until about 1834 when
2 CONT he temporarily removed to Texas.
2 CONT While sojourning in the Lone Star state, he enlised in the army of Houston, and was an
2 CONT active participant in that noted battle of the 21st of May, 1836 at which Santo Anna was
2 CONT triumpantly subdued - San Jacinto.
2 CONT After an honerable discharge from Houston's service Schooley continued his travels,
2 CONT arriving at Green Bay per schooner fro Chicago in June the following year. Here he obtained a
2 CONT "sit" under C. C. Henry O. Sholes, of the Green Bay Intelligencer, being known as a competent
2 CONT workman.
2 CONT Growing wearied of indoor like for the while, our typo friend embarked on a Durham boat
2 CONT and took at Athens, or Sauk-eer, as the Oshkoah settlement was then variously known. Liking its
2 CONT people, he entered a claim of 200 acres on the lake shore, adjoining the present Hospital farm on
2 CONT the Oshkosh side. He did not permanently locate on his claim for several years: erecting a log
2 CONT house, he kept bachelor hall therein and set type in The Intelligencer office, by turns, indulging in
2 CONT either pursuit as suited his somewhat restless temperament.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a time in the mid 1850s, Joseph went California to try his luck in the gold fields.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Schooley Obituary - Milwaukee Free Press  February 15, 1902
2 CONT The remains of Joseph L. Schooley, one of the oldest residents of the state, who died
2 CONT Wednesday at the home of his son, 353 Fifteenth Avenue, will be taken to Oshkosh this morning,
2 CONT the funeral being held tomorrow in that city. He was born in Easton, Pa., April 1, 1813. When 22
2 CONT years of age he joined an expedition from New York to Texas, and upon arriving there joined
2 CONT Houston's army, remaining with it until Texas was granted freedom by treaty. Returning to
2 CONT Pennsylvania two years later  in company of friends he started for Wisconsin via Buffalo and the
2 CONT Great Lakes, arriving in Green Bay, June 12, 1837, after a trip of three months. His first visit to
2 CONT Milwaukee was made over 50 years ago, when he drove an ox team over land from Fond du lac to
2 CONT meet friends who had come west to join their fortunes with his in the territory of Wisconsin. He was
2 CONT married September 20, 1848 to Sarah Brooks, who is at present very low with pneumonia and too
2 CONT ill to be informed of the death of her husband. Mr. Schooley has lived at different times at Green
2 CONT Bay, Neenah, Oshkosh and Shawano.
