1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Phoebe Elizabeth Lough Boggs BIRTH	16 Jul 1872 [or July 16, 1873? see text below] Pendleton County, West Virginia, USA DEATH	27 Jun 1903 (aged 30) Clay County, West Virginia, USA BURIAL	 Boggs Family Cemetery Big O
2 CONC tter, Clay County, West Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/87335377
2 CONT
2 CONT First wife of Charles Anderson Boggs:
2 CONT
2 CONT West Virginia, Marriages Index, 1785-1971 Name: Charlie Or Charles Anderson Boggs Gender: Male Birth Date: abt 1877 Age: 21 Spouse's Name: Phoebe Elizabeth Lowe Spouse Gender: Female Spouse Age: 21 Marriage Date: 24 Aug 1898 Marri
2 CONC age Place: Clay County, West Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Phoebe Elizabeth Boggs, wife of Charles A. Boggs, whose maiden name was Phoebe Lough, was born at Fort Seybert, Pendleton County, W.Va., July 16, 1873, and died at her home at Big Otter, Clay County, W.Va., June 27, 1903, age 29 y
2 CONC ears, 11 months, and 11 days.
2 CONT
2 CONT This noble woman and her now bereaved husband were married August 24, 1898.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 14 she confessed Christ as her Saviour and united with the M.E. Church, South, in which she remained and exemplified a consistent Christian life until transferred by death to the church triumphant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Boggs was the splendid keeper of an excellent home, into which she by her congenial spirit made it a great pleasure for her friends to enter, and from which we always departed feeling that we had a cordial welcome awaiting 
2 CONC our return.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was of a meditative turn of mind; hence her words always seemed well considered. She loved her Saviour, her church, her husband, her relatives, neighbors and friends, and in turn was loved by them.
2 CONT
2 CONT While she had not been in good health for sometime, her death came very unexpectedly to those even nearest to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the day of her death she had been more than usually active about household affairs, had taken supper as usual, and all had retired for the night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards midnight her struggle aroused her husband from sleep only to witness her death without hearing her speak a word.
2 CONT
2 CONT To the God of all comfort we commend the husband, father and sisters. "Blessed are the dead that die in the Lord."
2 CONT
2 CONT A very large concourse of people gathered at the Big Otter Church from which the funeral service was conducted by her pastor, Monday, June 28th, and followed her to the cemetery near by where she was buried according to the formul
2 CONC a of the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT {Abstracted from Pendleton County Obituaries}
