2 CONT Harrisonburg Daily News, Tuesday Morning, August 2, 1904
2 CONT
2 CONT Morgan G. Trumbo Dies At Charleston
2 CONT
2 CONT While Attending Session of West Virginia Legislature.
2 CONT
2 CONT Victim of Bright Disease
2 CONT
2 CONT Well Known merchant and Cattle Dealer at Brandywine--Was Representing Pendleton County in State Legislature.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morgan G. Trumbo, of Brandywine, W. Va., member of the West Virginia legislature and one of the most widely known and prosperous cattle men and merchants of Pendleton county, died at Charleston W. Va., yesterday morning, where he 
2 CONC was in attendance upon a special session of the legislature.  Mr. Trumbo had been absent from home only a few days when he was stricken with Bright's disease and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Saturday, July 24, Mr. Trumbo started to drive from his home in Brandywine to Harrisonburg for the purpose of taking a train here for Charleston.  He was not feeling well at the time and on arriving at the home of his friend Mr
2 CONC . Dabney Adams, who lives a short distance this side of Rawley Springs, he stopped with Mr. Adams, with whom he remained until Wednesday.  While there he complained of trouble with his stomach and consulted Dr. Turner, who prescri
2 CONC bed for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT On feeling somewhat better he left the Adams dwelling Wednesday morning and drove to Harrisonburg, where he took a train that afternoon to Charleston.  The next that was known of him, either here or at his home, was the intelligen
2 CONC ce of his death, which was conveyed to his family by a telegram yesterday by F. P. Moats speaker of the West Virginia House of Representatives.  Beyond the announcement of his death the telegram contained no particulars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later in the day a second dispatch was received from Charleston stating that death was due to Bright's disease and that the remains, accompanied by six members of the House of Representatives, would arrive in Harrisonburg this aft
2 CONC ernoon at 2:50 o'clock.  Mr. Trumbo was one of the most prominent men of his section and was almost as well known in Rockingham, where he ws a frequent visitor and owned considerable property, as he was in West Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in the mercntile business in Brandywine and was an extensive grazer and dealer in cattle, his many transactions in the latter bringing him into frequent touch with the stockmen of the Shenandoah Valley as well as those of t
2 CONC he border counties of his own state.  He was also the vice president of the Farmers' Bank of Pendleton, at Franklin, W. Va.
2 CONT
2 CONT In politics Mr. Trumbo was an ardent democrat and as such was elected two years ago to the legislature of his State as one of the representatives from pendleton county, having previously served for ten years as sheriff of that cou
2 CONC nty.  He was also an ex-Confederate soldier, having entered the army as a boy of sixteen when the Civil War began.  His army career was one of gallantry throughout and gave him an enviable standing with the people of his community
2 CONC , where he was the successful leader a few years ago in organizing a camp of Confederate Veterans.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an elder in the Brandywine Christian church Mr. Trumbo took an active interest in religious affairs, and his death will be a distinct loss to his church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased is survived by a widow who-- was a Miss Byrd, of South Branch, W. Va.--and two sons Alonzo and Cleveland Trumbo, and three daughters, Cleda, Shirley and Beulah.  He also leaves two brothers--John Trumbo, of Fauquier count
2 CONC y, and Wesley Trumbo, of near Brandywine, and one sister, Mrs. John Keister, also of near Brandywine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Trumbo was sixty years of age.
