1 NOTE {geni:about_me} m Walter Grant Simpson (3 Apr. 1923)
2 CONT
2 CONT Services Held Friday for Mrs. Beulah Simpson
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Beulah Trumbo Simpson, 70, of Brandywine, wife of Walter G. Simpson, died Tuesday May 15 while visiting the home of her son-in-law and daughter, the Rev. and Mrs. William C. Blackwell, at Fort Thomas, Kentucky.  Mrs. Simpson 
2 CONC had been in failing health for several years.   Mrs. Simpson was born December 23, 1891, the daughter of Morgan G. and Mary Catherine Byrd Trumbo, of Brandywine.  Both the Trumbo and Byrd families were leading pioneer families of 
2 CONC Pendleton County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are:  her husband, Walter G. Simpson, retired businessman of Brandywine;  a daughter, Mrs. Mary Helen Blackwell of Fort Thomas, KY.;  two brothers, Lon D. Trumbo, Brandywine, and Dr. G. C. Trumbo, Norfolk, VA;  two siste
2 CONC rs, Mrs. Harry (Shirley) Kiser, Greeley, Colorado, and Cleda Trumbo, Brandywine;  and two granddaughters, Mary Byrd and Catherine Coles Blackwell.  One brother, Aud B. Trumbo preceded Mrs. Simpson in death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Simpson attended Lynchburg (Virginia) College.  She was a member of the Brandywine Christian Church where she had served as organist, deaconess, clerk and treasurer.  In the earlier years of her marriage and while living in C
2 CONC larksburg and Scotsdale, Pa., she had served as deaconess in he Christian Churches of those communities.  Active in the Farm Women's Club in Brandywine, she was also the a past -president of the Pendleton County Farm Women's Club.
2 CONC   She was treasurer of Franklin Chapter 138, Order of the Eastern Star, and in former years had been a leader in 4-H Club work and a member of the Pendleton County Democratic Committee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Simpson were married in the the Christian Church of Cumberland, Maryland on April 3, 1923.  Mrs. Simpson was widely known as a gracious hostess and frequently entertained friends and her husband's business associates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Services were conducted Friday May 18, at the Brown Funeral Chapel, Franklin, by the Rev. Dr. John F. Nelson of Clarksburg assisted by the Rev.Mr. Stanley Johnson of Brandywine and the Rev. Mr. Claude MacDonald of Strasburg, Virgi
2 CONC nia.  Interment was in Pine Hill Cemetery at Brandywine.
