2 CONT
2 CONT It was owned by several people with Taney connections. This will be a rather long description, therefore. The Zirkles were among the early settlers in the valley of Virginia and in the region of Smith's Creek. Ludwig Zirkle came t
2 CONC o America from Germany about 1725, settling in Pennsylvania near Philadelphia. He had five sons and two daughters, at least. In about 1760-65, the five sons migrated to Virginia. Tow of them, Michael and Andrew, settled on the She
2 CONC nandoah River west of New Market; Lewis and Peter settled on Smith's Creek in what is now Rockingham County, VA. Peter soon moved on to Botetourt County, VA. Lewis Ziekle, Sr. (1740-1815) settled on Smith's Creek about 1760. He wa
2 CONC s a tanner and miller, was prosperous and accumulated a large estate, owning at the time oif his death some 1500 acres or more. This is the house he built; it stands near the foot of a hill on the south side of the entrance road t
2 CONC o Endless Caverns; the Caverns were a part of his estate. The house was built of stone and a log or frame part added later; the frame part was later torn down. Lewis married Mary Magdaline Roush and had four sons and eight daughte
2 CONC rs. They were: George who married Catherine WEST; John who married Regina DAUGHERTY; Lewis Jr. who married Nancy CALDWELL; Daniel who married Nancy PHILLIPS; Eve who married Jacob Roller; Elizabeth who married Phillip Arey; Susan 
2 CONC who married John Higgs; Margaret who married George Rosenberger of "Rosendale"; Sarah who married Thomas ROBINSON; Rachel who married William Pence; Catherine who married Abram PETERS; Mary M. who married Henry HUFF. On the north 
2 CONC side of the road to the Caverns, near this house, stands "Rosendale", the old home of George Rosenberger, who married Margaret Zirkle. This place was also part of Lewis Zirike's estate. Lewis Zirkle Jr. had his home nearby also on
2 CONC  the east side of Smith's Creek. The old Zirkle Mill (which I posted a long time ago) stood on Lewis Jr.'s homestead. Daniel Zirkle had the tan yard located at his home site Lewis Zirkle donated land for the first Lutheran church 
2 CONC built in New Market in 1793. This house has 2 chimneys and is 1 1/2 stories. It has 9 rooms and 9-ft. ceilings. A closed stairway leads to the upper level. When this picture was taken, it was one of the oldest in the Valley of Vir
2 CONC ginia. 
