2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Peter Circle along with his four brothers served in the American Revolution. Peter joined the Dunmore County Militia under the command of Captain Jacob Holman, serving in 1775, and has been recognized as a Patriot by the Daug
2 CONC hters of the American Revolution'''.
2 CONT ''
2 CONT '''Peter Zirkle Sr.''' aka Peter Circle
2 CONT * BIRTH - 1741 - Telford, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT * DEATH  Sep 1818 (aged 76–77) - Alleghany County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL - Locust Bottom Cemetery, Botetourt County, Virginia, USA 
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID - 62619257 · View Source
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/62619257/peter-zirkle
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Peter Circle was born in 1741 in Telford just north of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He was the eighth child of Johann Ludwig Zirckel and Maria Eva Bear. Peter’s father Ludwig died in Telford in 1747 when Peter was about 6 years old
2 CONC . His widowed mother Eva moved with her five sons and at least two of her daughters to an area known as the “Forest” or “Forestville” in Shenandoah County, Virginia in the early 1750s when Peter was in his early teens.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family apparently owned land in this area and Peter’s mother Eva obtained a land grant from King George II in 1755 for an additional 54 acres adjoining her own land. Peter’s brother Andrew obtained land and he and his brothers
2 CONC  constructed a grist mill around 1760 in Forestville. This mill was operated by various members of the Zirckel family for years and is still in existence today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter at some point changed the spelling of his name to Circle. In 1773 he bought land southeast of Forestville on Holman’s Creek, in what is now Shenandoah County, Virginia. He received a land grant for 78 acres from Lord Fairfax
2 CONC  in 1777. He sold land on Holman’s Creek in 1782 and 1783.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Circle along with his four brothers served in the American Revolution. Peter joined the Dunmore County Militia under the command of Captain Jacob Holman, serving in 1775, and has been recognized as a Patriot by the Daughters
2 CONC  of the American Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter married Irene Frene “Fanny” Meyer in 1773 in Shenandoah County, Virginia. She had been born in 1751 in Botetourt County. They eventually had a family of six sons and five daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter and his family moved to Botetourt County, Virginia around 1784. He owned land on the James River about 4 miles south of Iron Gates. He became an extensive landowner and is said to have owned 1086 acres. He was a farmer, he r
2 CONC an a tavern (ordinary), a still, and held slaves. There is a record of Peter Circle buying 149 acres of land adjoining his land on the north side of the James River in Botetourt County, Virginia from the Mayer family for $700. The
2 CONC y lived in communities which were primarily German, having been settled by German immigrants. Many continued to speak German and they usually had their own churches and schools. Peter Circle was said to have organized his church i
2 CONC n Botetourt County in 1786.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter and Fanny Circle had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Andrew Circle, born 1773 in Shenandoah County, Virginia 2. Catherine Circle, born 1775 in Virginia 3. Peter Circle, born 1775 in Augusta County, Virginia 4. Emanuel Circle, born January 23, 1782 in Valley, Botetourt, Virginia 5
2 CONC . Mary Magdeline Circle, born 1783 in Allegheny County, Virginia 6. John Lewis Circle, born September 13, 1784 in Botetourt County, Virginia 7. Elizabeth Circle, born May 7, 1789 in Rockingham County, Virginia 8. John Circle, born
2 CONC  1793 in Botetourt County, Virginia 9. Mathias Circle, born 1797 in Botetourt County, Virginia 10. Francis Circle, born 1800 in Virginia 11. Sarah Circle, born May 26, 1803 in Shenandoah County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Circle died in Botetourt County, Virginia in September of 1818 at the age of 77. His wife Irene Frene (Meyer) Circle died at an unknown date sometime after this. They were both buried in the Locust Bottom Cemetery in Botetou
2 CONC rt County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Circle left a will dated July 25, 1818 (about two months before his death) in which he left considerable land to his family. Some of the highlights are as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT “To the widow, Fanny Circle, all household and kitchen furniture, two horses and harness complete and farming utensils, three cows, 5 sheep, and ten hogs”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To son Andrew Circle, one-half of the plantation he now lives on”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To son Mathias Circle, plantation I now live on”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To my three sons, Emanuel Circle, John Lewis Circle, and John Circle, parts of my plantation”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To my son-in-law, Jacob Nicely, half of the plantation he now lives on”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To my son, Peter Circle II, the place he now lives on”.
2 CONT
2 CONT “To my three sons-in-law, Isaac France, John Nicewonger, and Christly Dasher, each $500 in cash”.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Circle’s farm was located along the James River in Botetourt County in Locust Bottom. He had been one of the people instrumental in building the Locust Bottom Church which was surrounded by his property and it was to the adj
2 CONC oining cemetery that he was brought for burial. The plantation was about 1500 acres and his house was located on the road (now Route 622) southeast of the church. On November 15, 1797 Peter had been granted a license to keep an or
2 CONC dinary (tavern/hotel/eatery/stable) at his house. The plantation was large and he did have slaves. Some of his children were already living on separate farms on his land and by his Will he distributed his plantation to his childre
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT When land in Virginia became limited many of Peter’s descendants migrated west and became some of the early settlers in Ohio, Indiana, Illinois, Missouri, and Kansas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter’s sons Emanuel and Andrew came west and settled in Clark County, Ohio near what is now Lawrenceville. One source said they had freed their slaves before they came to Ohio. The 1830 U.S. Census does list one free colored pers
2 CONC on living in Emanuel’s household of eleven people in Clark County.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Peter’s death, several of his sons remained on the plantation. The 1850 and 1860 U.S. Censuses included slave schedules that listed the slave owner’s names and each slave’s age, sex, and color but not their names. In 1850 Pe
2 CONC ter’s son John Lewis Circle had five slaves and in 1860 nine. Peter’s grandson, John L. Circle, Jr. had two slaves in 1850 and by 1860 had five on one farm and four on a second farm. Peter’s son Mathias had two slaves in 1850 and 
2 CONC his grandson Daniel (John L. Circle’s son) had two slaves. Mathias died in 1857 but on the 1860 U.S. Census, his widow Mary was listed as owning seven slaves. Daniel was not listed in 1860.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter’s daughters Magdaline and Sarah stayed in Virginia. Daughter Elizabeth went to Montgomery County, Ohio and Francis went to Preble County, Ohio. Peter’s son John went to Carroll County, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT All eleven of Peter and Irene Frene Circle’s children lived to adulthood and married and there are many descendants spread out across the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Civil War, Charles E, Daniel S, George, Jacob H, Madison, Samuel, and Thomas Circle all served in the Union Army and William Circle served in the U.S. Calvary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew P. Circle who was the son of Mathias Circle and the grandson of Peter Circle served in the Confederate Army and was killed while serving in 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT Peter (Zirkle) Circle was born in 1741 in Telford, Pennsylvania, to Maria Eva Bear*, age 32, and Johann "Lodowick" Ludwig Zirkle*, age 36.
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT Peter married Ferne Irene "Fanny" Meyer in New Market, Virginia, in 1773 when he was 32 years old.[1]
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Peter (Zirkle) Circle died in 1818 in Botetourt, Virginia, when he was 77 years old.[1]
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT Peter is buried in Locust Bottom Cemetery in Botetourt County, Virginia.[2][1]
2 CONT Military
2 CONT Peter (Zirkle) Circle served the fight for independence, joining the Dunmore Militia, which later became the 8th Virginia Regiment of Foot of the Continental Line under Colonel (later Brig Gen) John Muhlenberg.[1]
2 CONT Property
2 CONT Peter bought land 1773, southeast of Forestville on Holmans Creek.[1]
2 CONT Peter received in 1777 from Lord Fairfax, a land grant of 78 acres.
2 CONT Peter was an extensive land owner on the James River about four miles south of Iron Gates, Virginia.[1]
2 CONT Will
2 CONT Peter had a will dated 25 July 1818, which was probated in October 1818.[1]
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 1.5 1.6 Harpine, J William. Zirkle Family History, page 135. Self Published; Harrisburg, VA; 1963 (PDF copy in possession of Arthur Van Riper.
