1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Christian Niswanger, father of Jacob, signed a petition with Jost Hite and others to the Governor of Pennsylvania May 10, 1729, and asked for protection from the Indians, but soon moved to Virginia, when Jost Hite 
2 CONC got large land grants in what was then Orange Co., Virginia, but later changed to Frederick Co..  Family tradition says that Christian Niswanger’s wife, Maria Magdelena, was a granddaughter of Hans Herr, the leader of the Mennonit
2 CONC es to Pennsylvania in 1710.  On March 24, 1736 Jost Hite and Anna Maria (his first wife) deeded to Christian Niswanger 435 acres on the Shenandoah River (Orange Co. Deed Book 1, page 436), but Christian must have died in 1738, for
2 CONC  on Feb. 22, 1738/9 Jost Hite deeded the same 435 acres to Jacob Niswanger, who was Christian’s eldest son, confirming the later in the property (Orange Co. Deed Book 1, page 80.  On September 10, 1741 Jost Hite and Maria Magdalen
2 CONC a, the relict of Christian Neuschwanger, entered into a marriage contract “with the knowledge and consent of our friends, children, and relations”  (Frederick Co., Va Deed Book 1, page 241-242).  This contract was signed by John, 
2 CONC Jacob, Isaac, and Joseph Hite, sons of Jost Hite by his first marriage, and by Jacob Niswanger, obviously the only son of Christian Niswanger who was of age.” In a second book “German Origins of Jost Hite – Virginia Pioneer” by He
2 CONC nry Jones, Ralph Connor and Klaus Wust, the follow was submitted:
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 24:  “Amidst the early stage of conflict with Fairfax, tragedy hit the Hite home.  Anna Maria Hite died only 52 years old in 1739, soon after she and Jost had become godparents of Anna Maria, daughter of John and Sarah Hite. 
2 CONC  The widower was left with two children under ten years of age.  Two years later, Jost Hite married Maria Magdalena, the widow of Christian Neuschwanger, his long time friend and neighbor both in Pennsylvania and Virginia.  Prior 
2 CONC to the wedding a marriage contract was entered into on November 10, 1741.  It contains an inventory of her property and provided that in the event of her death on half was to go to her new husband and the other half to her own hei
2 CONC rs.”  
