1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Patrick Bernard Delany (The Future of the Electric Telegraph) - Born Kings County, Ireland, 1845; educated private and parochial schools Ireland and America; learned telegraphy in Hartford, Conn., at an early age; 
2 CONC occupied various positions from office boy to superintendent of lines; expert practical operator, newspaper correspondent, editor and writer; inventor anti induction cables, synchronous multiplex telegraphy, automatic systems for 
2 CONC ocean cables, and rapid machine telegraphy for land lines, comprising in all about 150 patents; member and ex vice-president American Institute Electrical Engineers; member Franklin Institute, from which he has received the Elliot
2 CONC t-Cresson gold medal and the John Scott (legacy) medal for his inventions; awarded gold medal and diploma at International Inventions Exhibition, London, 1885, for synchronous multiplex telegraph system, afterwards adopted and now
2 CONC  extensively used by British post-office. Mr. Delany has just perfected a system of automatic telegraphy capable of transmitting and plainly recording 3000 words per minute over a single wire, and with which he proposes the inaugu
2 CONC ration of letter telegraphy, or a virtual carrying of the United States mail between cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ The Engineering Magazine, Devoted to Industrial Progress, Vol. X, by The Engineering Magazine Co., New York, publ. 1896.
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT Associate of Edison Is Dead
2 CONT South Orange, N.J., Oct. 20 - Patrick B. Delany, 79, inventor, for many years an associate of Thomas E. Edison, died last night after ten days' illness from heart trouble.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Hamilton Evening Journal [Hamilton, Ohio], Mon., 20 Oct 1924
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT Inventor Dies at Age of 80
2 CONT South Orange, N. J., Oct. 20 - Patrick Bernard Delaney, 80, inventor of numerous telegraph appliances, and former associate of Thomas Edison, died at his home here today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Delaney was the inventor of anti-induction cables; synchronous multiplex telegraphy, by which it became possible to transmit six messages over one wire at the same time; automatic systems for ocean cables; rapid machine telegraphy
2 CONC  for land lines, and a number of other well known electrical devices.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Modesto Evening News [California], Mon., 20 Oct 1924, pg. 2
