1 TITL Col
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Englebert Glover Ovenshine, or E.G. as he preferred, was born in 1871, the son of General Samuel Ovenshine and Sallie (Thompson) Ovenshine, in Fort Hays, Kansas, where his father was stationed at the time. Born into a military fam
2 CONC ily, E.G. was moved around when he was growing up.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 2, 1894, E.G. and his younger brother, Alexander, followed their family tradition and enlisted in the United States Army, at Fort Sheridan, Illinois. E.G. served in the Spanish-American War while with the 16th Infantry in
2 CONC  the Philippines. He was next on duty in 1905 at Fort Snelling in Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 6, 1910, E.G. married Ethel Florence Keyes, the daughter of Alexander Brooks and Virginia Beaubien (Maxwell) Keyes, in Fort Des Moines, Polk County, Iowa. Ethel's father was also a career military man. He died in 1909, a 
2 CONC year before Ethel married. E.G. and Ethel would have no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1918 E.G. and Ethel are found immigrating into San Francisco, California, from Honolulu, Hawaii, most likely from a Pacific posting.
2 CONT
2 CONT They are living in New York state in 1920 and by 1925 they were in Rhode Island. In 1928, after 34 years of service, E.G. retired from the Army. He and Ethel would live in Massachusetts, California and Washington, District of Colu
2 CONC mbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethel passed away in 1955 at 70 years old while E.G. lived on until 1968 when he died at 96 years of age. Their bodies are buried in the Arlington National Cemetery in Arlington, Arlington County, Virginia.
