1 OCCU Co. K, 62nd Regiment, Virginia Mounted Infantry
1 TITL Pvt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Washington Eye BIRTH	12 Jul 1840 Moyers, Pendleton County, West Virginia, USA DEATH	17 Sep 1914 (aged 74) Deer Run, Pendleton County, West Virginia, USA BURIAL	 Eye Family Cemetery Deer Run, Pendleton Count
2 CONC y, West Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/140437795/william-washington-eye
2 CONT
2 CONT William Washington was the second oldest son of William and Letitia Bodkin. He grew up in the Moyers area of Pendleton County, WV. As a young man he joined the confederate forces in the Valley of Virginia 1 Jul 1862, and served as
2 CONC  a Private in the mounted infantry. He served first in the 1st Co and 2nd Co K and then transferred to Co I in the 62nd Virginia Infantry. At the end of the war he married Susan Elizabeth Sinnett. Both are descendants of revolutio
2 CONC nary war patriots. At the end of the war he purchased 300 acres in the Deer run area once VA and after the war WV, for the sum of $150 or 50 cents an acre. On this land he raised a family of 10 children, the youngest being Wade Wh
2 CONC itney Eye. He was a very tall, strong man and in that era of time, all work was manual and he was well known as having incredible strength. He made a Walnut Wood Rocking Chair for each child, (he is sitting in one in the picture h
2 CONC olding his favorite dog.) He also made a walnut table, a walnut chest and other furniture for his 12 room farm home. He was the father of 7 beautiful girls and of course along with that came the many suitors for their hands in mar
2 CONC riage. As horses and buggies were the transportation of the time, many stories were told about how he would hide the suitors horses or take the wheels off the buggy or climb to the top of the pear tree and tie their saddles to the
2 CONC  top. He was also firm to disagree with a neighbor if the occasion required. One neighbor was very reluctant to build the line fence. So he moved in 10 feet to his side and built his own line fence. The neighbor responded by doing
2 CONC  the same and built his line fence. The space in between was referred to as devil's lane. Two of his grandsons were often called upon to repair the fence in devil's lane. William Washington attended all the Pendleton Co Confederat
2 CONC e Reunions until his death in 1914. In his obit it stated that "Each veteran as they walked by the open casket dropped in a sprig of evergreen as was the custom indicating true friendship."
2 CONT
2 CONT Eye, William W. BATTLE UNIT NAME: 62nd Regiment, Virginia Mounted Infantry SIDE: Confederacy COMPANY: 2 K SOLDIER'S RANK IN: Private SOLDIER'S RANK OUT: Private ALTERNATE NAME: FILM NUMBER: M382 ROLL 18 PLAQUE NUMBER: NOTES: Battl
2 CONC e Unit Note - (1 Regiment Virginia Partisan Rangers. 62 Regiment Virginia Partisan Rangers. 62 Regiment Virginia Infantry. 62 Regiment Virginia Cavalry. Imboden's Regiment Partisan Rangers.)
