1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor #: A088914
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew Patton BIRTH	1730 DEATH	3 May 1803 (aged 72–73) Clark County, Kentucky, USA BURIAL	 Patton Graveyard Clark County, Kentucky,
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/89021454/matthew-patton
2 CONT
2 CONT Died at age 73 years
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew's was the only grave in this area on the farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a possibility there are two Matthew Pattons. I do not know if this information is correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew Patton (1730-1803)
2 CONT
2 CONT Members of the Dyer family (his wife was of these Dyers) he moved over the mountains to the valley of the South Branch of the Potomac River. Finding the area unhealthful, they moved father up the valley and settled in Sweedland Di
2 CONC strict near the site of the future Fort Seybert, close to what is Franklin, West Virginia. Fort Seybert was one of a chain of forts laid out by Major George Washington for the protection of the frontier. Matthew was Lt. of Foote i
2 CONC n the Virginia Militia in 1753 and was promoted to Captain in 1768. He was commissioned Justice of the Peace in 1761. He furnished supplies to the army during Dunmore's Rebellion in 1774. Matthew was the first importer of English 
2 CONC cattle of the shorthorn breed to Augusta County. His name appears in Eckenrode's List of Revolutionary War Soldiers from Virginia. He is considered a Patriot because he furnished supplies to the American troops. His son, Matthew, 
2 CONC Jr. was also a soldier in the Rev. War. He moved to Kentucky, about 1793, taking with him a few head of the celebrated "Patton" stock, so well known in Ross County, Kentucky in later years. This information contributed by Glenda P
2 CONC atton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I have found there is no Ross Co., Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT  Parents:
2 CONT   John J Patton (1689 - 1757)
2 CONT   Sarah Rogers Patton (1690 - 1752)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT   Roger Patton (1751 - 1811)*
2 CONT
2 CONT  Siblings:
2 CONT   Matthew Patton (1730 - 1803)
2 CONT   Samuel Patton (1732 - 1799)*
2 CONT   Lydia Patton Alkire (1739 - 1798)*
2 CONT   William Patton (1742 - 1818)*
2 CONT   Agnes Patton Patterson (1765 - 1843)**
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT **Half-sibling
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Patton Graveyard  Clark County Kentucky, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Edit Virtual Cemetery info [?]
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Stonefinder-Deb Record added: Apr 23, 2012  Find A Grave Memorial# 89021454 http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=89021454
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~dmorgan/genealogy/chapter1.html
2 CONT
2 CONT >Matthew Patton was the step-father of Capt. George Barber.  He lived next to the Watkins family in Johnston County, North Carolina in 1767, and lived next to Capt. George Barber and Reese Watkins in Wilkes County, Georgia.  Matth
2 CONC ew Patton died in Wilkes County, Georgia in 1806, and two of the executors of his will were Capt. George Barber and Christopher Orr.  Matthew Patton had been a witness to the will of Christopher's father, Robert Orr, in Johnston C
2 CONC ounty, North Carolina in 1779.2  This same Christopher had married Martha, the  daughter of Reese Watkins.  Reese Watkins was the older brother of Margaret Watkins.3  
