4 CONT There is a possibility there are two Matthew Pattons. I do not know if this information is correct.
4 CONT
4 CONT Matthew Patton (1730-1803)
4 CONT
4 CONT Members of the Dyer family (his wife was of these Dyers) he moved over the mountains to the valley of the South Branch of the Potomac River. Finding the area unhealthful, they moved father up the valley and settled in Sweedland Di
4 CONC strict near the site of the future Fort Seybert, close to what is Franklin, West Virginia. Fort Seybert was one of a chain of forts laid out by Major George Washington for the protection of the frontier.
4 CONT Matthew was Lt. of Foote in the Virginia Militia in 1753 and was promoted to Captain in 1768. He was commissioned Justice of the Peace in 1761. He furnished supplies to the army during Dunmore's Rebellion in 1774. Matthew was the 
4 CONC first importer of English cattle of the shorthorn breed to Augusta County. His name appears in Eckenrode's List of Revolutionary War Soldiers from Virginia. He is considered a Patriot because he furnished supplies to the American 
4 CONC troops. His son, Matthew, Jr. was also a soldier in the Rev. War.
4 CONT He moved to Kentucky, about 1793, taking with him a few head of the celebrated "Patton" stock, so well known in Ross County, Kentucky in later years. This information contributed by Glenda Patton. 
4 CONT
4 CONT I have found there is no Ross Co., Kentucky
4 CONT
