1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Coil/Kile (1730 Palatinate, Germany - 1794 • Pendleton County, Virginia) was the son of Valentine "Felty" Coil/Goil (1690–1767) and Margaret Unknown.  Valentine "Felty" Coil was a Palatine German immigrant[1
2 CONC ] to Pennsylvania HIs name was sometimes spelled "Geil" and "Coile". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Valletin Geil and his family arrived at the port in Philadelphia on September 15, 1749 on the ship Edinburgh from Rotterdam Holland and settled in the colony of Virginia.  The passenger list showed the passengers were from the Pal
2 CONC atinate which is modern day Germany.  George is mentioned in his father's will proved on 19 Aug 1765[2].  According to family tradition, George married Maria Johanna Hevener (1747–1837) but his will refers to his wife "Hannah".  C
2 CONC hildren Include:  George  Jacob  John  Catharine  Elizabeth  Barbara (7 Jan 1778 Pendelton County, Virginia - 11 APR 1855 Concord, Ross, Ohio) married Jacob Fisher Sr (13 JAN 1774 Pennsylvania - 20 FEB 1866 Concord, Ross, Ohio)  H
2 CONC annah 
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate[3]: George Coil; Probate Place: Pendleton, Commonwealth of Virginia; Item Description Wills, Vol 1, 1788-1801, pg 57-59:  Will Book I pp. 57-59: 18 February 1793 WILL of George COIL. Weak in body. Son George Coyle: tract f
2 CONC ormerly belonging to John Gordoin plus a survey adjoining the same, and a tract on the Dry Fork of Cheat, formerly belonging to Joseph Summerfield. Son Jacob Coyle: 200 acres at the lower end of the 500 acre tract I live on, adjoi
2 CONC ning his brother George, plus 100 acres on the Dry Fork of the Cheat below Colonel Nivell's survey. Son John Coyle: 300 acres of the tract I live on, plus a small survey on Switzer's Gap, adjoining George Lough. Wife Hannah Coyle:
2 CONC  to retain lands and chattels during her widowhood or until the marriage of eldest son George, provided he married before Jacob reaches age 21, After son John reaches 21, all the personal property to be sold. Wife Hannah: Negro we
2 CONC nch Soot and all her increase plus Negro boy Tom. Daughter Catherine: mulattor girls Cate and Mime, plus mare, saddle 150 pounds (currency). Daughter Elizabeth Coyle: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Negro girl Lucy, 100 pounds currency. Daughter Barbara: Negro girl Abbe, horse, saddle, 200 pounds currency. Daughters Mary and Hannah [youngest daughters, elswhere in the text]: horse, saddle, 250 pounds currency each. Son George
2 CONC  Coyle: Negro boy Saul, Son Jacob, Negro boy Jesse. Son John, Negro boy Gib. Inheritances to daughters to be paid as per: George to pay 100 pounds currency to Mary from his part of the land and 150 pounds currency from his part of
2 CONC  the movables. Jacob to pay 50 to Hannah from his land, the rest from the movables. John at 21 is to provide decent maintenance for my widow, if she is still unmarried, and also for unmarried daughters, this not to diminish their 
2 CONC legacies. Executors: Wife Hannah, Adam Moser, son George Coyle. Signed George [C] Coyle. Witnesses Robt. Poage, John Hopkins, [illegible german script], George Baxter, Proved by Hopkins and Ruchard Wilson [sic], 3 March 1794  pp. 
2 CONC 60-61 3 March 1794 BOND of Adam MOSER, Hannah COIL and George COIL, executors of George Coil. Signed Adam [A] Moser, Hannah [+] Coil, George Geil, Peter Hull, Jacob Conrad and Ulrich Conrad. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:  19 Jun 1775[4] - Augusta County, Virginia Land Patent. George Coil; Property On South Branch of Potowmack. Remarks: Property part of patent to Robert Green, 12 Jan 1746. Proved, 20 Jun 1775, court being adjourned from Fort
2 CONC  Dunmore.  A tale passed down in the Coil family, which has been confused with young Felty's captivity, is the story of Jacob and George's capture by the Indians. This incident may have happened while the two were young men in the
2 CONC re twenties. They had been captured by a band of Indians and were tied with ropes. Jacob gnawed through his ropes releasing himself where he then untied his brother. They dressed themselves as Indians and disappeared into the wood
2 CONC s escaping from their Indian captors. Jacob was said to be a large man with great strength and when his brother tired he carried him. George left his brother hidden in some brush and continued on arriving safely at a frontier stat
2 CONC ion. The guards didn't recognize at first since he was dressed like an Indian, but once he called out to the guard they realized who he was and let him into the fort. Afterward Jacob and a few others returned to George's hiding pl
2 CONC ace and carried him off to safety.  Sources  ↑ Strassburger, Ralph Beaver. Pennsylvania German Pioneers: A Publication of the Original Lists of Arrivals in the Port of Philadelphia from 1727 to 1808. Edited by William John Hinkle.
2 CONC  Norristown, PA: Pennsylvania German Society, 1934. 3 vols. Vols. 1 and 3 reprinted by Genealogical Publishing Co., Baltimore. 1964. Reprint 1983. Vol. 1, 1727-1775. p. 402 ↑ Virginia Land, Marriage, and Probate Records, 1639-1850
2 CONC  ↑ West Virginia, Wills and Probate Records,1724-1985. ↑ This land record was originally published in "Chronicles of the Scotch-Irish Settlement in Virginia, 1745-1800. Extracted from the Original Court Records of Augusta County" 
2 CONC by Lyman Chalkley Grady Loy, firsthand knowledge. Click the Changes tab for the details of edits by Grady and others.  Find A Grave, Memorial# 95695766. 
2 CONT
