0 @N6000000001723436483@ NOTE Roger Dyer was an early settler of Augusta County, Virginia, in an area which later became Pendleton County, West Virginia. He and his son William were killed in the Indian massacre at Fort Seybert, West Virginia on April 28, 1758
1 CONC .
0 @N6000000004086787126@ NOTE Roger Dyer was killed by Indians.
0 @N6000000001723436325@ NOTE William and his father were killed in the Indian massacre at Fort Seybert, West Virginia on April 28, 1758.
0 @N6000000004972873343@ NOTE Taken From: http://www.migrations.org/individual.php3?record=28513
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Magdalene (Miller) Mallow (1717-1777)was taken captive in Delaware massacre at Fort Seybert, VA in 1758. She traveled with her son Adam Mallow(1751-1841), to Indian Camp, located in what is now, Concord township, Ross County,
1 CONC  Ohio. While there she delivered son Henry Mallow (1758-1834), believed to be first white captive born to this camp. Several years later she and Henry were released due to captive trade agreement, but Adam Mallow was forced to sta
1 CONC y behind. Several years later Adam Mallow returned to home In VA.
0 @N6000000004972850407@ NOTE "Adam Mallow's Migration" by Michelle Morris, Researcher
1 CONT Found at: http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/ViewStory.aspx?tid=4057813&pid=-1656183209&did=6aabb07d-ecbd-41b5-aaa9-cdd266dfc3e8&src=search
1 CONT
1 CONT Adam Mallow, son of Johann Michael and Mary Mallow was taken captive by Delaware Indians in massacre at Fort Seybert, VA in 1758. Adam Mallow and mother Mary Mallow traveled with Indians to camp at Concord Twp., Ross County, OH. M
1 CONC ary Mallow delivered first white child born in native captivity, son Henry Mallow. Several years later family returned home to Pendleton County, VA. In 1804 Adam Mallow was awarded 1,000 acres per Virginia Military Warrant, and re
1 CONC turned to area of captivity, bringing wife, Sarah Bush, son, Adam Mallow Jr., and wife Phoebe Dice, and children. They remained in Concord township, Ross County, OH, until their deaths. This family is buried at original site of Ma
1 CONC llow-Hegler Cemetery, on Plano. Rd, Concord Twp., Ross County, Ohio.
1 CONT
1 CONT ******************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT Taken From: http://genforum.genealogy.com/mallow/messages/119.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Second proven child of Johann Michael Mallo was Johann Adam Mallo (later known as Adam Mallow). Birth date accepted by his descendants is 6 Oct 1751. We know for a fact that he was, indeed, 21 years old prior to 16 Mar 1773 when h
1 CONC e was appointed co-administrator of his father's estate. I have copies of all the probate records for Michael's estate.
1 CONT Note: The accepted birth date is therefore likely and that Adam was not named in honor of his paternal grandfather - as was customary. It MAY be that he wss named in honor of Johann Adam Moser/Mosser who was the husband of Anna Ma
1 CONC ria Moser, Adam's sister's baptismal sponsor.
1 CONT Adam's grandchildren believed that he was born near the South Branch of the Potomac in then Augusta Co, VA (now Pendleton Co, WV) and that is likely.
1 CONT
1 CONT ******************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT From p.237 of The Official Roster of the Soldiers of the American Revolution Buried in the State of Ohio, Vol. II
1 CONT
1 CONT MALLOW, ADAM, Ross co
1 CONT
1 CONT Pvt Va as ranger and scout. Was b 10-6-1751. Native of Pendleton co Va; son of Michael and Mary Mallow; mar Sarah Bush (b 5-16-1753 d 2-4-1850) dau of Lewis Bush and sister of Michael and Leonard. Chldr: Adam Jr (Maj in 1812 mar P
1 CONC hoebe Dice) ; Eve mar Wm Dice; Henry (Capt in 1812 mar Sarah Popejoy) ; Margaret mar Jacob Carr. Soldr d 11-27-1841 Concord twp Ross co; bur Mallow or Hegler cem of W side of N Fork of Paint Cr abt 1 mi N of mouth of Herrod Cr. In
1 CONC script on monument “In memory of Adam Mallow, Sr who departed this life Nov 27 1841 ae 90 yr 1 mo 21 das.” Wife bur at his side (east end of cem) Grave mkd by family; Kodak of stone filed. As a chld taken by parents to Valley of S
1 CONC  Branch of Potomac nr Fort Seybert, Pendleton co W Va (now). In Apr 1758 captured by Indians who brot him to village of old Chillicothe, now Frankfort Ross co O. In 1764 ae 13 returned to his parents. Called John Brown by Indians;
1 CONC  in 1806 returned and bought land whr he had lived in captivity. Was in battle Pt Pleasant. Ref Biog Hist Pickaway, Madison and Fayette co; Centen Hist of Ross co; Clara G Mark, Historian of Bush Fam Asso. Rept by Jacobus Westerve
1 CONC lt chpt.
0 @N6000000004229902741@ NOTE REFN: 10002
0 @N6000000003772585161@ NOTE His estate settled in Nelson Co., KY 1783-1800; moved from Hampshire Co., VA to Wellsburg, WVA in 1767
0 @N6000000004879185987@ NOTE     All 1700-1800 COX  information is influenced by research done by "FILSON Club" Louisville KY and by family records in later years. See also "Coxes of Cox's Creek"
1 CONT
1 CONT In 'Virginia's Colonial Soldiers'  under Dunmore's War 1774 names of soldier on pay rolls at Pittsburg:
1 CONT    Capt.George rogers Clark Roll: GABRIEL COX, Lt.;  George Cox, Sgt.  also Joseph Cox
1 CONT    Capt.Abraham Teagarden's roll: David Cox
1 CONT
1 CONT THERE APPEARS TO BE A NAME CHANGE IN THE EARLY 1700'S as the COX name does not go back any farther and a family with the SAME GIVEN names with the last name of COCK does not appear after the middle 1700's (see disk 27 World Family
1 CONC  Tree #975, & #38 tree#537, & #39 tree# 1511)
0 @N6000000004879185960@ NOTE David was active in the affairs of Yohogania Co. (now Washington Co.PA.) and served in Dunmore's War. He was a Lieutenant in the Militia in 1778.
1 CONT David, Col.Isaac, and Benjamin Cox went to KY in the spring of 1780. 
1 CONT David with brothers John, Gabriel, George, and Benjamin apraised estates, laid out roads, and served on Juries in KY.
1 CONT resided on 'Cox's Creek' since 1780.. See the essay "The Coxes of Cox's Creek" by Evelyn C. Adams
1 CONT He was a Captain in Nelson Co. Militia
0 @N6000000004881466796@ NOTE 'They had a home in Wellsburg, Brooke Co., W.VA. George Cox and his sister, Ann Cox McMahan Worrell stayed in W.VA. when others in the family moved to Kentucky. George said that he was born in Maryland and brought slaves with him 
1 CONC to Virginia. He was said to be an educated man.' 
1 CONT  will written July 10, 1837...
0 @N6000000004881466844@ NOTE David, Col.Isaac, and Benjamin Cox went to KY in the spring of 1780. 
1 CONT  'a great personal tragedy befel the Cox brothers  while decending the Ohio river in the flatboat hearing wild turkey calls, lured a canoe close enough to the bank for an indian ambush. Joseph Cox was killed and one of the slaves.
1 CONC  Benjamin Cox was able to paddle away though severely wounded in the arm.'
1 CONT David with brothers John, Gabriel, George, and Benjamin apraised estates, laid out roads, and served on Juries in KY. quoted from "Coxes of Cox's creek"
1 CONT had permanent home on Bullskin Creek in Shelby Co., KY
0 @N6000000004881466804@ NOTE record is obscure
0 @N6000000004881466826@ NOTE died en-route to Kentucky- un married. 'a great personal tragedy befel the Cox brothers  while decending the Ohio river in the flatboat hearing wild turkey calls, lured a canoe close enough to the bank for an indian ambush. Joseph
1 CONC  Cox was killed and one of the slaves. Benjamin Cox was able to paddle away though severely wounded in the arm.'
0 @N6000000004881466562@ NOTE David, Col.Isaac, and Benjamin Cox went to KY in the spring of 1780. 
1 CONT David with brothers John, Gabriel, George, and Benjamin apraised estates, laid out roads, and served on Juries in KY.
1 CONT Will of Isaac Cox  written sept. 5,1786.  wife Mary to have the plantation where they live. to free the slaves and provide schooling if young.  Friend, Davis,Isaac-sons of David Cox-L500 due from Jamers Nourse due 1789.  Elizabeth
1 CONC  Cox, Sarah Cox Suzannah Cox- daughters of Benjamin Cox -1500 acres of land on Clear Creek of Rough Creek. Nancy Cox-daughter of Gabriel Cox-200 acres on Nolin entered in name of Nicholas Lewis and assigned to me. To brother Johna
1 CONC than Cox-430 acres near the mouth of Cox's Creek. John and Isaac Cox-sons of George Cox L100 each. Benjamin Cox, son of John Cox-L200 from last payment of Alexander Skinner and Gilbert Imlay, due 1788. Brother Gabriel Cox- half of
1 CONC  3000 acres on Noide(including mouth of Salt River) and 2485 acres in knobs near Long Lick both entered in name of Henry Banks. Brother David- half of 1835 acres in partnership with John May. Brother Benjamin- half of land in part
1 CONC nership with Benjamin Netherland and John Card, about 2000 acres. ..Executors, wife Mary, brother Gabriel Cox, and friend James Rogers.
0 @N6000000004881466834@ NOTE twin to Benjamin
1 CONT "came to KY in 1785-86 perhaps with Gabriel. Purchased land and inherited several hundred acres from the 2 Isaac Coxes. In 1808 he was living near Benjamin and Polly in Shelby Co. where many of his wife's relatives ,the Lemens, al
1 CONC so lived. In 1808 Isabella inherited land from her father and until 1815 they sold a number of tracts. they then joined Poly and John Lemen in Knox Co., Indiana." quoted from "Coxes of Cox's creek"
0 @N6000000004881466781@ NOTE ran away from home at age 18 and became an indian scalper...killed by mistake dressed in indian garb returning to his forted home in Hampshire Co.(Filson Society)
0 @N6000000004548272250@ NOTE In the War of the Polish Succession (1733-35), which took place primarily in Italy and the Rhineland, France, Spain, Bavaria, and the Kingdom of Sardinia fought against Austria, which was supported by Russia. The pretext for the w
1 CONC ar was Austro-Russian intervention in Poland in support of Frederick Augustus II of Saxony, who was elected (1733) king of Poland (as Augustus III) by a minority of the Polish nobles. His rival was Stanislaw I, who was elected kin
1 CONC g by most of the nobles and was supported by Louis XV of France, his son-in-law. Louis, however, was more interested in gaining Lorraine than in helping Stanislaw, who fled in 1733.
1 CONT The war ended with a series of settlements finalized in the Treaty of Vienna (1738). Lorraine was given to Stanislaw for life in return for abdicating the Polish throne and was to revert to France after his death. Francis Stephen,
1 CONC  duke of Lorraine (later Holy Roman Emperor Francis I ) was compensated with Tuscany, which was added to Austrian possessions because he had married (1736) the Austrian archduchess Maria Theresa.
1 CONT With Francis Stephen removed from Lorraine, France recognized the Pragmatic Sanction that made Maria Theresa heir to Holy Roman Emperor Charles VI. The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies went to a branch of the Spanish Bourbons; they in 
1 CONC turn gave up Parma and Piacenza to Charles. In addition, the War of the Polish Succession established Russia as a dominant influence in Polish affairs.
1 CONT Anna M. Cienciala
1 CONT Bibliography: Davies, Norman, God's Playground: A History of Poland, vol. 1 (1982); Gieysztor, Aleksander, et al., eds., History of Poland, trans. by K. Cekalska, et al., (1968); Reddaway, W. F., et al., eds., The Cambridge Histor
1 CONC y of Poland, 2 vols. (1941; repr. 1971).
1 CONT (c) 1996 Grolier, Inc.
1 CONT **************************************************************************
